Answer: If you do  $\require{cancel}x=5\tan\theta$  and  $\mathrm dx=5\sec^2\theta\,\mathrm d\theta$ , then  $\int\frac1{\sqrt{25+x^2}}\,\mathrm dx$  becomes $$\int\frac{\cancel5\sec^2\theta}{\sqrt{\cancel{25}(1+\tan^2\theta)}}\,\mathrm d\theta=\int\frac{\sec^2\theta}{\sqrt{\sec^2\theta}}\,\mathrm d\theta=\int\sec\theta\,\mathrm d\theta.$$ Now, use the fact that  $\int\sec\theta\,\mathrm d\theta=\log|\sec(\theta)+\tan(\theta)|+C$ . 
